{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794561144446,"sessionID":"ses_e2bd34d8effe9ZeAv34d1wqUl4","part":{"partID":"prt_1d42df502001qDJomvEMdlTk4h","sessionID":"ses_e2bd34d8effe9ZeAv34d1wqUl4","messageID":"msg_1d42d9b5c0012fFSy212OTuztc","type":"tool","id":"call_42dc1e6c0f01416bb0d7cf7d","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md, lines 1-295\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-create-readme\n3: description: Author sk-doc folder, code-folder and skill/project READMEs from local evidence.\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 1.3.16.0\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Create README (workflow)\n9: \n10: `create-readme` is the README authoring packet in the `sk-doc` family. It writes current-state folder READMEs from local evidence, using packet-local templates under `assets/` and shared create-quality-control validators under `../shared`.\n11: \n12: ---\n13: \n14: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n15: \n16: ### Activation Triggers\n17: \n18: Use this packet when the request involves:\n19: \n20: - Creating or refreshing a folder `README.md`.\n21: - Documenting a project, skill, feature, component or source-code directory.\n22: - Writing developer orientation for a code folder, including topology, boundaries, entrypoints and validation.\n23: - Running `/create:readme`.\n24: \n25: Keyword triggers: `create readme`, `/create:readme`, `readme`, `folder readme`, `write readme`, `README.md`, `front-page overview`, `code folder readme`.\n26: \n27: ### When NOT to Use\n28: \n29: Use another `sk-doc` packet when:\n30: \n31: - The user wants to audit, validate, score, or optimize an existing README or another existing document without authoring or refreshing it. Use `create-quality-control`.\n32: - The user wants to scaffold a skill, agent, command, benchmark package, feature catalog, testing playbook, flowchart, or changelog. Use `create-skill`, `create-agent`, `create-command`, `create-benchmark`, `create-feature-catalog`, `create-manual-testing-playbook`, `create-flowchart`, or `create-changelog`.\n33: - The target is not markdown.\n34: - The folder is self-explanatory and a parent README or inline comments already give enough orientation.\n35: \n36: This is a nested workflow packet under the `sk-doc` parent hub. It owns README authoring only. It does not carry `graph-metadata.json`; advisor identity, skill graph metadata and cross-packet routing live at the `sk-doc` hub root.\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n41: \n42: `sk-doc` routes README authoring here: the request names a README, a folder readme, a code-folder readme or `/create:readme`.\n43: The hub's mode signal selects this packet.\n44: Three artifacts route here: a general README, a code-folder README and a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n45: The artifact decides the template before the folder decides the sections.\n46: \n47: Authoring starts from the matching template: `assets/readme-template.md` for a general README, `assets/readme-code-template.md` for a source-code folder, and `assets/architecture-template.md` for a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n48: `references/README.md` is the fallback route map when the artifact type or folder purpose is unclear.\n49: \n50: When nothing matches, report `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with the router's disambiguation checklist instead of guessing.\n51: \n52: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n56: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n57: \n58: Every run follows one operating model, whatever the artifact:\n59: \n60: 1. Read local evidence first: the target folder, nearby docs, package files, config files and existing commands. Never document unconfirmed files, commands, APIs or metrics.\n61: 2. Route by artifact type and folder purpose (`ROUTER.md` Section 3) to pick the output and template.\n62: 3. Draft current-state content only, in the smallest useful shape: a general README (Section 5) or a code-folder README (Section 6).\n63: 4. Copy the matching template as a scaffold, remove unused sections and put orientation first.\n64: 5. Validate the authored markdown and resolve local links before delivery (Section 7).\n65: \n66: Both README shapes share this lifecycle. The sections below give the detailed steps for each.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 4. README AUTHORING WORKFLOW\n71: \n72: 1. Identify the target folder, audience and README type before drafting.\n73: 2. Read the target folder contents, nearby documentation, package files, config files and existing commands.\n74: 3. Do not invent commands, files, APIs, metrics, features or capabilities.\n75: 4. Choose the smallest useful README shape. Use a general README for project, skill, feature and component roots. Use a code-folder README for source-directory orientation.\n76: 5. Write current-state documentation only. Use stable file paths, commands, feature names and source links instead of packet IDs, phase IDs, migration history or temporary planning labels.\n77: 6. Put orientation first: what this is, who it is for and how to use or navigate it.\n78: 7. Apply progressive disclosure: title and tagline answer \"what is this\", overview helps the reader decide relevance, quick start appears only when there is a runnable path and detailed reference comes later.\n79: 8. Use two-tier voice for project, skill and feature READMEs: plain narrative for overview and usage, precise reference tables for options, configuration and tool signatures.\n80: 9. Use technical reference voice for component and code-folder READMEs.\n81: 10. Copy the relevant template only as a scaffold. Remove unused sections instead of leaving placeholders.\n82: 11. Use relative links for local documents and verify that links resolve from the README location.\n83: 12. Test documented commands when feasible. If a command cannot be tested locally, mark it clearly as an example.\n84: 13. Show expected output for verification commands.\n85: 14. Validate the authored markdown before delivery.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 5. GENERAL README OUTPUT SHAPE\n90: \n91: A general README answers two questions: what this is, and how someone uses or navigates it.\n92: \n93: Use only the sections that fit the audience.\n94: \n95: | Section | Include When | Content |\n96: | --- | --- | --- |\n97: | Overview | Always | Purpose, audience, main value and prerequisites |\n98: | Key Statistics | Useful facts help readers decide scope | Counts, version, supported modes or limits |\n99: | How This Compares | Readers may confuse this with nearby tools | Short comparison table |\n100: | Quick Start | Readers need to run or try something | Short setup, verification and first use |\n101: | Features | Capabilities need explanation | Feature groups, behavior notes and examples |\n102: | Requirements | Runtime, tool or service needs exist | Minimum versions, permissions and external services |\n103: | Structure | Navigation matters | Directory tree and key files |\n104: | Configuration | Settings exist | Config files, options, defaults and env vars |\n105: | Usage Examples | Patterns are easier shown than described | Simple and advanced examples with expected results |\n106: | Troubleshooting | Users can hit known issues | What they see, cause and fix |\n107: | FAQ | Repeated questions exist | Short answers only |\n108: | Related Resources | Links exist | Grouped skills, documents, commands or external references |\n109: \n110: General README format rules:\n111: \n112: - H1 is a plain title.\n113: - Add a one-sentence blockquote tagline immediately after the H1.\n114: - H2 headings are numbered and ALL CAPS, for example `## 1. OVERVIEW`.\n115: - Numbered H3 and H4 subsections inside feature sections are ALL CAPS, for example `### 3.1 TECHNICAL REFERENCE`.\n116: - Unnumbered H3 and H4 headings use Title Case.\n117: - Name the feature or artifact in its heading: `### Spec Kit: a written record of every change`, not `### Powered by Spec Kit`.\n118: - Emoji use follows two registers. The repository front-page README may use decorative emoji on section headings. Every other README uses semantic emoji only (✅ ❌ ⚠️ 🔒 🚨) for rules and status markers.\n119: - Give every item in an inventory a description. A list of bare names says nothing about when to use each one.\n120: - Explain in short paragraphs of short sentences, about 10-15 words each and about 60 words a paragraph. Use bullets only for items a reader scans or compares: commands, options, steps and parallel facts. A run of bullets that each hold a sentence of explanation reads better as a paragraph.\n121: - When installing takes more than a few steps, a front-page README keeps only the minimum commands and points to a root install document, such as `INSTALL-SKILLED.md`.\n122: - Use `---` horizontal rules between H2 sections.\n123: - When a README spaces its sections with `&nbsp;` lines, put one above every H2, H3 and H4 heading. Leave it out only where a heading directly follows another heading, so sibling sections never get different gaps.\n124: - Do not add a Table of Contents.\n125: - Do not add `<!-- ANCHOR -->` navigation comments.\n126: - Use tables for comparisons, file maps, configuration, options and troubleshooting.\n127: - Use fenced code blocks with language tags.\n128: \n129: Frontmatter is optional for normal project READMEs and recommended for skill or system documentation that should be discoverable.\n130: \n131: ```yaml\n132: ---\n133: title: \"[DOCUMENT_TITLE]\"\n134: description: \"[One-sentence description]\"\n135: trigger_phrases:\n136:   - \"[search phrase]\"\n137:   - \"[routing phrase]\"\n138: ---\n139: ```\n140: \n141: ---\n142: \n143: ## 6. CODE-FOLDER README OUTPUT SHAPE\n144: \n145: Create a code-folder README when a developer needs local orientation before editing the directory.\n146: \n147: Include details when relevant:\n148: \n149: - High-level purpose: what the folder owns.\n150: - Architecture diagram: how callers, handlers, adapters and storage relate.\n151: - Package topology: layers, zones and allowed dependency direction.\n152: - Directory tree with only important files.\n153: - Key files and responsibilities.\n154: - Boundaries: what this folder may import, export or own.\n155: - Data or control flow through important paths.\n156: - Public entrypoints, exports or commands.\n157: - Validation and test commands.\n158: - Related documents.\n159: \n160: Use this section model:\n161: \n162: | Section | Include When | Notes |\n163: | --- | --- | --- |\n164: | Overview | Always | One or two paragraphs about current responsibility |\n165: | Architecture Diagram | Helpful for dispatch, pipelines, adapters or layered flows | Prefer zones and arrows over prose |\n166: | Package Topology | The folder has layers, zones or import rules | Show dependency direction clearly |\n167: | Directory Tree | Always for multi-file folders | Show only important files |\n168: | Key Files | Responsibilities are split | Table with file and role |\n169: | Boundaries | Import rules or ownership matter | State allowed and disallowed edges |\n170: | Data or Control Flow | Runtime paths matter | Keep to the main path |\n171: | Entrypoints | The folder exposes APIs, commands or exports | Include function names, files or CLI commands |\n172: | Validation | Tests or checks exist | Use commands that work from repo root |\n173: | Related | Nearby docs exist | Link parent, sibling and architecture docs |\n174: \n175: The general README format rules at `General README format rules:` above bind code-folder READMEs.\n176: That covers numbered H2 headings, ALL-CAPS H2 casing, `---` separators and language-tagged fences.\n177: It also covers no Table of Contents and no anchor-comment navigation.\n178: The blockquote tagline is the exception.\n179: It does not bind code-folder READMEs.\n180: This is a cross-reference to the single authoritative rule block, not a second list.\n181: \n182: The Directory Tree rule is shape-conditional and script-implementable:\n183: \n184: 1. Count the target folder's immediate subdirectories.\n185: 2. When that count is greater than zero, include a fenced Directory Tree because the folder has nesting or layering. The validator emits `code_folder_directory_tree` when it is missing.\n186: 3. When that count is zero, a complete `CONTENTS`, `FILES`, `KEY FILES`, `WHAT IS IN THE PACKAGE` or `STRUCTURE` inventory naming every direct file other than the README satisfies the navigation requirement. A file named in a fenced structure tree counts. The validator emits `code_folder_flat_inventory` when that table is absent or incomplete.\n187: \n188: Both branches are named rules. A reviewer must not replace the subdirectory-count condition with a judgement about whether a tree feels useful.\n189: \n190: Code-folder README rules:\n191: \n192: - Document current behavior only.\n193: - Use code terms exactly as they appear in files.\n194: - Prefer file paths, exported names and commands over narrative history.\n195: - Keep diagrams small enough to read in a terminal.\n196: - State allowed dependency direction when the folder has layers.\n197: - Do not force Quick Start, Features, FAQ or Troubleshooting unless the directory has runnable commands, meaningful capabilities or known reader problems.\n198: - The code-folder scaffold omits the general blockquote tagline by design. Frontmatter remains optional, and when present it stays before the H1.\n199: \n200: ---\n201: \n202: ## 7. VALIDATION\n203: \n204: Run shared validation on authored markdown when feasible:\n205: \n206: ```bash\n207: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/check_authored_name_kebab.py <artifact-path-or-slug>\n208: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/validate_document.py <path>\n209: ```\n210: \n211: Run the authored-name check after resolving the output path and before document validation. `README.md` is a tool-mandated exemption; every other authored name must pass the kebab-case rule.\n212: \n213: Use quick validation or structure extraction when appropriate:\n214: \n215: ```bash\n216: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/quick_validate.py <path>\n217: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/extract_structure.py <path>\n218: ```\n219: \n220: As an internal authoring check after creating or refreshing READMEs, the packet-local inventory script can verify repository coverage from the repo root:\n221: \n222: ```bash\n223: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/scripts/audit_readmes.py --repo-root . --validator .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/validate_document.py\n224: ```\n225: \n226: Use `--json-out`, `--markdown-out` or `--inventory-out` when the user needs artifacts. Treat broken references, missing key artifact coverage and validator blocking errors as findings to fix or report.\n227: \n228: Pre-publish checks for READMEs:\n229: \n230: - README type is correct. Code folders use the code-folder shape.\n231: - Included sections match the audience and task.\n232: - No empty sections or placeholders remain.\n233: - Section numbers are sequential.\n234: - Local links use correct relative paths.\n235: - Commands were tested or clearly marked as examples.\n236: - Expected outputs are shown for verification commands.\n237: - Code blocks specify language tags.\n238: - HVR passes.\n239: \n240: ---\n241: \n242: ## 8. WRITING RULES\n243: \n244: ### ALWAYS\n245: \n246: - Read the target folder and nearby docs before writing.\n247: - Choose the README type before choosing a template.\n248: - Keep READMEs current-state and reader-focused.\n249: - Use relative links for local documents.\n250: - Use fenced code blocks with language tags.\n251: - Test documented commands when feasible.\n252: - Show expected output for verification commands.\n253: - Use tables for file maps, options, comparisons and troubleshooting.\n254: - Keep `README.md` and `SKILL.md` casing intact.\n255: \n256: ### NEVER\n257: \n258: - Add a Table of Contents or anchor-comment navigation.\n259: - Leave template placeholders in the final document.\n260: - Document commands, files or features not confirmed from workspace or user evidence.\n261: - Cite mutable packet numbers, phase IDs or migration bookkeeping in durable README content.\n262: - Force Quick Start, Features, FAQ or Troubleshooting into a code-folder README when the folder only needs orientation.\n263: - Create `graph-metadata.json` in this packet.\n264: \n265: ### HUMAN VOICE RULES\n266: \n267: - No em dashes. Use commas, periods or colons.\n268: - No semicolons. Split the sentence or use \"and\".\n269: - No Oxford comma in inline lists.\n270: - No setup phrases.\n271: - No banned words from the HVR reference.\n272: - Prefer active voice and direct verbs.\n273: - Use analogies only when they clarify narrative sections. Do not use analogies in component or code-folder READMEs.\n274: \n275: ### ESCALATE IF\n276: \n277: - The target folder purpose or audience is unclear.\n278: - The README would require product, brand or public-facing claims that are not in the repo.\n279: - Documenting a step needs secrets, paid services, destructive operations or external accounts.\n280: - Validation fails in a way that requires changing code or configuration outside the requested document.\n281: \n282: ---\n283: \n284: ## 9. OVERFLOW REFERENCES\n285: \n286: The core workflow lives in this `SKILL.md`. Use these files only for deep overflow detail, exhaustive scaffolds, edge cases and long examples:\n287: \n288: - `assets/readme-template.md` for the full fillable general README scaffold.\n289: - `assets/readme-code-template.md` for the full fillable code-folder scaffold and diagram examples.\n290: - `assets/architecture-template.md` for a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`: the fixed eight-section system document.\n291: - `references/README.md` for the overflow route map that indexes the `readme/` reference group.\n292: - `references/readme/` for extended README type, voice, writing-pattern and quality detail across three single-concern files.\n293: - `../shared/references/core-standards.md` for shared document formatting rules.\n294: - `../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md` for the full Human Voice Rules.\n295: - `../shared/references/evergreen-packet-id-rule.md` for current-state documentation rules.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md, lines 1-295\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-create-readme\n3: description: Author sk-doc folder, code-folder and skill/project READMEs from local evidence.\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 1.3.16.0\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Create README (workflow)\n9: \n10: `create-readme` is the README authoring packet in the `sk-doc` family. It writes current-state folder READMEs from local evidence, using packet-local templates under `assets/` and shared create-quality-control validators under `../shared`.\n11: \n12: ---\n13: \n14: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n15: \n16: ### Activation Triggers\n17: \n18: Use this packet when the request involves:\n19: \n20: - Creating or refreshing a folder `README.md`.\n21: - Documenting a project, skill, feature, component or source-code directory.\n22: - Writing developer orientation for a code folder, including topology, boundaries, entrypoints and validation.\n23: - Running `/create:readme`.\n24: \n25: Keyword triggers: `create readme`, `/create:readme`, `readme`, `folder readme`, `write readme`, `README.md`, `front-page overview`, `code folder readme`.\n26: \n27: ### When NOT to Use\n28: \n29: Use another `sk-doc` packet when:\n30: \n31: - The user wants to audit, validate, score, or optimize an existing README or another existing document without authoring or refreshing it. Use `create-quality-control`.\n32: - The user wants to scaffold a skill, agent, command, benchmark package, feature catalog, testing playbook, flowchart, or changelog. Use `create-skill`, `create-agent`, `create-command`, `create-benchmark`, `create-feature-catalog`, `create-manual-testing-playbook`, `create-flowchart`, or `create-changelog`.\n33: - The target is not markdown.\n34: - The folder is self-explanatory and a parent README or inline comments already give enough orientation.\n35: \n36: This is a nested workflow packet under the `sk-doc` parent hub. It owns README authoring only. It does not carry `graph-metadata.json`; advisor identity, skill graph metadata and cross-packet routing live at the `sk-doc` hub root.\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n41: \n42: `sk-doc` routes README authoring here: the request names a README, a folder readme, a code-folder readme or `/create:readme`.\n43: The hub's mode signal selects this packet.\n44: Three artifacts route here: a general README, a code-folder README and a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n45: The artifact decides the template before the folder decides the sections.\n46: \n47: Authoring starts from the matching template: `assets/readme-template.md` for a general README, `assets/readme-code-template.md` for a source-code folder, and `assets/architecture-template.md` for a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n48: `references/README.md` is the fallback route map when the artifact type or folder purpose is unclear.\n49: \n50: When nothing matches, report `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with the router's disambiguation checklist instead of guessing.\n51: \n52: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n56: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n57: \n58: Every run follows one operating model, whatever the artifact:\n59: \n60: 1. Read local evidence first: the target folder, nearby docs, package files, config files and existing commands. Never document unconfirmed files, commands, APIs or metrics.\n61: 2. Route by artifact type and folder purpose (`ROUTER.md` Section 3) to pick the output and template.\n62: 3. Draft current-state content only, in the smallest useful shape: a general README (Section 5) or a code-folder README (Section 6).\n63: 4. Copy the matching template as a scaffold, remove unused sections and put orientation first.\n64: 5. Validate the authored markdown and resolve local links before delivery (Section 7).\n65: \n66: Both README shapes share this lifecycle. The sections below give the detailed steps for each.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 4. README AUTHORING WORKFLOW\n71: \n72: 1. Identify the target folder, audience and README type before drafting.\n73: 2. Read the target folder contents, nearby documentation, package files, config files and existing commands.\n74: 3. Do not invent commands, files, APIs, metrics, features or capabilities.\n75: 4. Choose the smallest useful README shape. Use a general README for project, skill, feature and component roots. Use a code-folder README for source-directory orientation.\n76: 5. Write current-state documentation only. Use stable file paths, commands, feature names and source links instead of packet IDs, phase IDs, migration history or temporary planning labels.\n77: 6. Put orientation first: what this is, who it is for and how to use or navigate it.\n78: 7. Apply progressive disclosure: title and tagline answer \"what is this\", overview helps the reader decide relevance, quick start appears only when there is a runnable path and detailed reference comes later.\n79: 8. Use two-tier voice for project, skill and feature READMEs: plain narrative for overview and usage, precise reference tables for options, configuration and tool signatures.\n80: 9. Use technical reference voice for component and code-folder READMEs.\n81: 10. Copy the relevant template only as a scaffold. Remove unused sections instead of leaving placeholders.\n82: 11. Use relative links for local documents and verify that links resolve from the README location.\n83: 12. Test documented commands when feasible. If a command cannot be tested locally, mark it clearly as an example.\n84: 13. Show expected output for verification commands.\n85: 14. Validate the authored markdown before delivery.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 5. GENERAL README OUTPUT SHAPE\n90: \n91: A general README answers two questions: what this is, and how someone uses or navigates it.\n92: \n93: Use only the sections that fit the audience.\n94: \n95: | Section | Include When | Content |\n96: | --- | --- | --- |\n97: | Overview | Always | Purpose, audience, main value and prerequisites |\n98: | Key Statistics | Useful facts help readers decide scope | Counts, version, supported modes or limits |\n99: | How This Compares | Readers may confuse this with nearby tools | Short comparison table |\n100: | Quick Start | Readers need to run or try something | Short setup, verification and first use |\n101: | Features | Capabilities need explanation | Feature groups, behavior notes and examples |\n102: | Requirements | Runtime, tool or service needs exist | Minimum versions, permissions and external services |\n103: | Structure | Navigation matters | Directory tree and key files |\n104: | Configuration | Settings exist | Config files, options, defaults and env vars |\n105: | Usage Examples | Patterns are easier shown than described | Simple and advanced examples with expected results |\n106: | Troubleshooting | Users can hit known issues | What they see, cause and fix |\n107: | FAQ | Repeated questions exist | Short answers only |\n108: | Related Resources | Links exist | Grouped skills, documents, commands or external references |\n109: \n110: General README format rules:\n111: \n112: - H1 is a plain title.\n113: - Add a one-sentence blockquote tagline immediately after the H1.\n114: - H2 headings are numbered and ALL CAPS, for example `## 1. OVERVIEW`.\n115: - Numbered H3 and H4 subsections inside feature sections are ALL CAPS, for example `### 3.1 TECHNICAL REFERENCE`.\n116: - Unnumbered H3 and H4 headings use Title Case.\n117: - Name the feature or artifact in its heading: `### Spec Kit: a written record of every change`, not `### Powered by Spec Kit`.\n118: - Emoji use follows two registers. The repository front-page README may use decorative emoji on section headings. Every other README uses semantic emoji only (✅ ❌ ⚠️ 🔒 🚨) for rules and status markers.\n119: - Give every item in an inventory a description. A list of bare names says nothing about when to use each one.\n120: - Explain in short paragraphs of short sentences, about 10-15 words each and about 60 words a paragraph. Use bullets only for items a reader scans or compares: commands, options, steps and parallel facts. A run of bullets that each hold a sentence of explanation reads better as a paragraph.\n121: - When installing takes more than a few steps, a front-page README keeps only the minimum commands and points to a root install document, such as `INSTALL-SKILLED.md`.\n122: - Use `---` horizontal rules between H2 sections.\n123: - When a README spaces its sections with `&nbsp;` lines, put one above every H2, H3 and H4 heading. Leave it out only where a heading directly follows another heading, so sibling sections never get different gaps.\n124: - Do not add a Table of Contents.\n125: - Do not add `<!-- ANCHOR -->` navigation comments.\n126: - Use tables for comparisons, file maps, configuration, options and troubleshooting.\n127: - Use fenced code blocks with language tags.\n128: \n129: Frontmatter is optional for normal project READMEs and recommended for skill or system documentation that should be discoverable.\n130: \n131: ```yaml\n132: ---\n133: title: \"[DOCUMENT_TITLE]\"\n134: description: \"[One-sentence description]\"\n135: trigger_phrases:\n136:   - \"[search phrase]\"\n137:   - \"[routing phrase]\"\n138: ---\n139: ```\n140: \n141: ---\n142: \n143: ## 6. CODE-FOLDER README OUTPUT SHAPE\n144: \n145: Create a code-folder README when a developer needs local orientation before editing the directory.\n146: \n147: Include details when relevant:\n148: \n149: - High-level purpose: what the folder owns.\n150: - Architecture diagram: how callers, handlers, adapters and storage relate.\n151: - Package topology: layers, zones and allowed dependency direction.\n152: - Directory tree with only important files.\n153: - Key files and responsibilities.\n154: - Boundaries: what this folder may import, export or own.\n155: - Data or control flow through important paths.\n156: - Public entrypoints, exports or commands.\n157: - Validation and test commands.\n158: - Related documents.\n159: \n160: Use this section model:\n161: \n162: | Section | Include When | Notes |\n163: | --- | --- | --- |\n164: | Overview | Always | One or two paragraphs about current responsibility |\n165: | Architecture Diagram | Helpful for dispatch, pipelines, adapters or layered flows | Prefer zones and arrows over prose |\n166: | Package Topology | The folder has layers, zones or import rules | Show dependency direction clearly |\n167: | Directory Tree | Always for multi-file folders | Show only important files |\n168: | Key Files | Responsibilities are split | Table with file and role |\n169: | Boundaries | Import rules or ownership matter | State allowed and disallowed edges |\n170: | Data or Control Flow | Runtime paths matter | Keep to the main path |\n171: | Entrypoints | The folder exposes APIs, commands or exports | Include function names, files or CLI commands |\n172: | Validation | Tests or checks exist | Use commands that work from repo root |\n173: | Related | Nearby docs exist | Link parent, sibling and architecture docs |\n174: \n175: The general README format rules at `General README format rules:` above bind code-folder READMEs.\n176: That covers numbered H2 headings, ALL-CAPS H2 casing, `---` separators and language-tagged fences.\n177: It also covers no Table of Contents and no anchor-comment navigation.\n178: The blockquote tagline is the exception.\n179: It does not bind code-folder READMEs.\n180: This is a cross-reference to the single authoritative rule block, not a second list.\n181: \n182: The Directory Tree rule is shape-conditional and script-implementable:\n183: \n184: 1. Count the target folder's immediate subdirectories.\n185: 2. When that count is greater than zero, include a fenced Directory Tree because the folder has nesting or layering. The validator emits `code_folder_directory_tree` when it is missing.\n186: 3. When that count is zero, a complete `CONTENTS`, `FILES`, `KEY FILES`, `WHAT IS IN THE PACKAGE` or `STRUCTURE` inventory naming every direct file other than the README satisfies the navigation requirement. A file named in a fenced structure tree counts. The validator emits `code_folder_flat_inventory` when that table is absent or incomplete.\n187: \n188: Both branches are named rules. A reviewer must not replace the subdirectory-count condition with a judgement about whether a tree feels useful.\n189: \n190: Code-folder README rules:\n191: \n192: - Document current behavior only.\n193: - Use code terms exactly as they appear in files.\n194: - Prefer file paths, exported names and commands over narrative history.\n195: - Keep diagrams small enough to read in a terminal.\n196: - State allowed dependency direction when the folder has layers.\n197: - Do not force Quick Start, Features, FAQ or Troubleshooting unless the directory has runnable commands, meaningful capabilities or known reader problems.\n198: - The code-folder scaffold omits the general blockquote tagline by design. Frontmatter remains optional, and when present it stays before the H1.\n199: \n200: ---\n201: \n202: ## 7. VALIDATION\n203: \n204: Run shared validation on authored markdown when feasible:\n205: \n206: ```bash\n207: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/check_authored_name_kebab.py <artifact-path-or-slug>\n208: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/validate_document.py <path>\n209: ```\n210: \n211: Run the authored-name check after resolving the output path and before document validation. `README.md` is a tool-mandated exemption; every other authored name must pass the kebab-case rule.\n212: \n213: Use quick validation or structure extraction when appropriate:\n214: \n215: ```bash\n216: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/quick_validate.py <path>\n217: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/extract_structure.py <path>\n218: ```\n219: \n220: As an internal authoring check after creating or refreshing READMEs, the packet-local inventory script can verify repository coverage from the repo root:\n221: \n222: ```bash\n223: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/scripts/audit_readmes.py --repo-root . --validator .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/validate_document.py\n224: ```\n225: \n226: Use `--json-out`, `--markdown-out` or `--inventory-out` when the user needs artifacts. Treat broken references, missing key artifact coverage and validator blocking errors as findings to fix or report.\n227: \n228: Pre-publish checks for READMEs:\n229: \n230: - README type is correct. Code folders use the code-folder shape.\n231: - Included sections match the audience and task.\n232: - No empty sections or placeholders remain.\n233: - Section numbers are sequential.\n234: - Local links use correct relative paths.\n235: - Commands were tested or clearly marked as examples.\n236: - Expected outputs are shown for verification commands.\n237: - Code blocks specify language tags.\n238: - HVR passes.\n239: \n240: ---\n241: \n242: ## 8. WRITING RULES\n243: \n244: ### ALWAYS\n245: \n246: - Read the target folder and nearby docs before writing.\n247: - Choose the README type before choosing a template.\n248: - Keep READMEs current-state and reader-focused.\n249: - Use relative links for local documents.\n250: - Use fenced code blocks with language tags.\n251: - Test documented commands when feasible.\n252: - Show expected output for verification commands.\n253: - Use tables for file maps, options, comparisons and troubleshooting.\n254: - Keep `README.md` and `SKILL.md` casing intact.\n255: \n256: ### NEVER\n257: \n258: - Add a Table of Contents or anchor-comment navigation.\n259: - Leave template placeholders in the final document.\n260: - Document commands, files or features not confirmed from workspace or user evidence.\n261: - Cite mutable packet numbers, phase IDs or migration bookkeeping in durable README content.\n262: - Force Quick Start, Features, FAQ or Troubleshooting into a code-folder README when the folder only needs orientation.\n263: - Create `graph-metadata.json` in this packet.\n264: \n265: ### HUMAN VOICE RULES\n266: \n267: - No em dashes. Use commas, periods or colons.\n268: - No semicolons. Split the sentence or use \"and\".\n269: - No Oxford comma in inline lists.\n270: - No setup phrases.\n271: - No banned words from the HVR reference.\n272: - Prefer active voice and direct verbs.\n273: - Use analogies only when they clarify narrative sections. Do not use analogies in component or code-folder READMEs.\n274: \n275: ### ESCALATE IF\n276: \n277: - The target folder purpose or audience is unclear.\n278: - The README would require product, brand or public-facing claims that are not in the repo.\n279: - Documenting a step needs secrets, paid services, destructive operations or external accounts.\n280: - Validation fails in a way that requires changing code or configuration outside the requested document.\n281: \n282: ---\n283: \n284: ## 9. OVERFLOW REFERENCES\n285: \n286: The core workflow lives in this `SKILL.md`. Use these files only for deep overflow detail, exhaustive scaffolds, edge cases and long examples:\n287: \n288: - `assets/readme-template.md` for the full fillable general README scaffold.\n289: - `assets/readme-code-template.md` for the full fillable code-folder scaffold and diagram examples.\n290: - `assets/architecture-template.md` for a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`: the fixed eight-section system document.\n291: - `references/README.md` for the overflow route map that indexes the `readme/` reference group.\n292: - `references/readme/` for extended README type, voice, writing-pattern and quality detail across three single-concern files.\n293: - `../shared/references/core-standards.md` for shared document formatting rules.\n294: - `../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md` for the full Human Voice Rules.\n295: - `../shared/references/evergreen-packet-id-rule.md` for current-state documentation rules."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794561144066,"end":1794561144446}}}}
